### 1. Legislative Level (Layer 1 $\rightarrow$ Layer 2)

The House Committee on Appropriations and the Senate Committee on Appropriations, through their Subcommittees on Financial Services and General Government (**FSGG**), dictate the operating ceilings of the SEC. Although the SEC is nominally self-funded through transaction fees collected under **Section 31 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934**, the **Anti-Deficiency Act (31 U.S.C. §§ 1341–1342)** prohibits the SEC from spending fee collections without explicit, authorized annual appropriations from Congress.

Legislative policy riders restricting the ingestion of Personally Identifiable Information (PII) into the **Consolidated Audit Trail (CAT)** and prolonged disputes over Self-Regulatory Organization (SRO) funding models historically impaired the SEC's market surveillance capacity:

* **August 2026 Governance Pivot:** SEC Chairman Paul Atkins directed staff to overhaul CAT governance by moving to rescind Rule 613, dismantle the industry-led SRO funding model (the CAT NMS Plan), and transition CAT operations directly under SEC budgetary authority funded via dedicated Section 31 fees by late 2027.
* **March 2026 Cost Reduction Amendment:** An interim amendment to the CAT NMS Plan reduced annual operating costs by $50–70 million by deleting historical data older than three years and halting interim lifecycle linkages across trade events.

### 2. Depository Level (Layer 2 $\rightarrow$ Layer 3)

All eligible securities in the U.S. market are immobilized within the **Depository Trust Company (DTC)** (designated as a SIFMU under Title VIII of Dodd-Frank, 12 U.S.C. § 5463) and registered on issuer stock books in the legal name of DTC's nominee partnership: **Cede & Co.**

* **Security Entitlements:** Under **Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) Article 8 (§§ 8-501 to 8-511)**, investors possess tiered "Security Entitlements" against their intermediaries rather than direct property titles to specific underlying shares.
* **Rehypothecation (SEC Rule 15c3-3):** Broker-dealers can legally re-pledge customer margin securities up to **140% of the customer's debit balance** to finance firm operations, enabling layered collateral re-use and reducing visibility into physical share location.
* **SAB 122 Rescission (January 23, 2025):** The SEC issued **Staff Accounting Bulletin No. 122**, which formally and retrospectively rescinded **SAB 121**. This removed the requirement for custodial banks to record client-safeguarded digital assets as on-balance-sheet liabilities, restoring Tier-1 institutional balance-sheet capacity within standard Basel III leverage ratios.

### 3. Clearing and Algorithmic Level (Layer 3 $\rightarrow$ Layer 4)

The National Securities Clearing Corporation (**NSCC**) and the Options Clearing Corporation (**OCC**) act as Central Counterparties (CCPs). The NSCC’s **Continuous Net Settlement (CNS)** engine nets gross member obligations by **97% to 99%** into a single net long or short position per CUSIP at the end of each day.

The total daily and intraday Clearing Fund formula enforced by the NSCC is defined as:


$$\text{Required Margin} = \text{VaR Charge} + \text{Gap Risk Measure} + \text{Excess Capital Premium (ECP)} + \text{Mark-to-Market} + \text{Special Charges}$$

* **Value-at-Risk (VaR):** Parametric and historical simulation models at a 99% single-tailed confidence interval over a 3-year lookback period.
* **Enhanced Gap Risk Measure (July 2026):** Assesses concentration risk haircuts (up to 100%) on the **sum of the gross market values of a member's two largest non-diversified net positions** across its entire portfolio. The rule mandates the **decomposition of Exchange Traded Funds (ETFs)** into their underlying equity constituents to eliminate proxy-hedging loopholes.
* **Excess Capital Premium (ECP):** Governed by NSCC Procedure XV. Imposed when a member's calculated volatility margin component exceeds its excess regulatory net capital under **SEC Rule 15c3-1**, applying a non-linear multiplier:

$$\text{ECP Surcharge} = \left( \frac{\text{Calculated VaR}}{\text{Excess Net Capital}} - 1 \right) \times \text{VaR Component}$$


* **The Accord and GSP Payments:** When equity options are exercised or assigned at the OCC, the underlying physical stock delivery transfers to the NSCC's CNS engine. Under the restated OCC-NSCC Accord, the OCC transfers a **Guaranty Substitution Payment (GSP)** to the NSCC to cover incremental counterparty risk if a common member defaults.

