## Section II. T+1 Settlement Compression Mechanics and Systemic Vulnerability Vectors

On **May 28, 2024**, the SEC implemented amended **Rule 15c6-1**, transitioning U.S. markets to a **T+1 settlement cycle**. International markets (EU, UK, and Switzerland) are coordinating parallel T+1 migrations scheduled for **October 11, 2027**.

```
========================================================================================================================
 T+2 vs. T+1 POST-TRADE OPERATIONAL TIMELINE COMPARISON
========================================================================================================================

 T+2 SETTLEMENT TIMELINE (Legacy):
 [Trade Date (T)] ───────────────> [T+1: Allocations & Confirmations] ───────────────> [T+2: Settlement]
 |<───────────────────────────── 12-Hour Operational Reconciliation Window ──────────────────────────>|

 T+1 SETTLEMENT TIMELINE (Current):
 [Trade Date (T)] ──> [21:00 ET: Affirmation Cutoff] ──> [23:30 ET: CNS Night Cycle] ──> [T+1: NSS Settlement (16:15 ET)]
 |<── 2-Hour Window (83% Compression) ──>|
========================================================================================================================

```

### Critical Operational Failure Modes Under T+1:

1. **83% Reconciliation Window Compression:** Slashes post-trade reconciliation from 12 hours to 2 hours. Inaccurate **Standing Settlement Instructions (SSIs)** immediately cause un-affirmed trades to drop out of automated CNS netting, forcing manual, gross matching on $T+1$.
2. **Cross-Border Foreign Exchange (FX) Dislocation:** U.S. trade affirmation occurs at 9:00 PM ET (03:00 AM Central European Time). European and Asian trading desks are closed, preventing access to **Continuous Linked Settlement (CLS)** for guaranteed same-day currency conversion and forcing foreign asset managers into expensive bilateral FX trades or overdraft facilities to meet $T+1$ USD cutoffs.
3. **Securities Lending & Reg SHO Rule 204 Acceleration:** Loan recall notices must now be executed within hours of trade completion on $T$. Under **17 CFR § 242.204 (Reg SHO Rule 204)**, fail-to-deliver (FTD) positions at the NSCC must be resolved before market open on **$T+2$**.

