### Core Audit Conclusions

1. **Surveillance Data Compromise:** Material inaccuracies in Consolidated Audit Trail (CAT) filings and widespread Regulation SHO short-sale order-marking errors deprive regulators of real-time visibility into cross-market short concentration. The SEC's **August 2026 direct governance overhaul** is designed to centralize and remediate these multi-decade data hygiene failures.
2. **Custodial Decoupling & Rehypothecation:** While the **January 2025 rescission of SAB 121 (via SAB 122)** resolved balance-sheet capital bottlenecks for institutional digital asset custodians, traditional cash equities remain immobilized under **Cede & Co.**, enabling multi-layered rehypothecation (up to 140% under SEC Rule 15c3-3) and creating systemic locate vulnerabilities.
3. **SIFMU-Centric Risk Algorithms:** The risk architecture of the NSCC and OCC (including the **July 2026 Gap Risk methodology update** assessing the two largest net non-diversified positions and decomposing ETFs) functions to guarantee clearinghouse solvency at all costs. When combined with the **Excess Capital Premium (ECP)**, these formulas produce non-linear margin calls that rapidly exceed the regulatory net capital of clearing brokers.
4. **The Settlement Chokepoint & PCO Resolution:** Because Tier-1 Settling Banks operate under hard **Net Debit Caps ($1.8 billion maximum)** via the Federal Reserve's NSS, undercapitalized brokers cannot finance multi-billion-dollar margin calls during market gap events. To prevent triggering the **NSCC Rule 4 Loss Allocation Waterfall**, clearing brokers execute **Position Closing Only (PCO)** trading halts—disabling retail buy-side access to protect the financial integrity of the clearinghouse network.
